{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-e2-reviewer-fable-26","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:128c2bd7128bc67b5964","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","bounded_statement":"The selected result in results/report.md (exact two-sided binomial test, 25 right-arm entries of 36 pooled trials, p = 0.0288, declared a departure from chance at the 0.05 level) is produced by entering all 36 rows — six repeated runs from each of the six whip spiders, whose independent unit is animal_id per data-description.md — as independent Bernoulli observations into scipy.stats.binomtest, a procedure that assumes row independence. With only 6 independent units and per-animal right-arm shares spanning 0.333 to 1.000, animal-level analyses of the same data (one-sample t-test on the six per-animal shares: t ≈ 1.94, df = 5, p ≈ 0.11; sign test on majority choice: 4 right vs 1 left with one tie, p = 0.375) do not reach the 0.05 level, so the stated statistical conclusion is not supported when the declared independent unit is respected.","root_cause":"workflow/analysis.py pools every row of data/input.csv into a single exact binomial test with n = len(trials) = 36 (p_value = binomtest(n_right, n_trials, NULL_RATE, ...)), treating each of the six repeated runs per animal as an independent observation even though data-description.md designates animal_id (6 animals) as the independent unit. The repeated within-animal measurements are correlated (per-animal shares range 0.333–1.000), so the pooled test overstates the effective sample size and understates the p-value, and the report's [selected-result] claim of significance at 0.05 depends on this pseudoreplication.","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description explicitly designates animal_id as the independent-unit column and states each row is one run by one whip spider.","observed_value":"Independent unit column: animal_id; one row = one T-junction run","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":17,"end_line":19,"quoted_text":"One row is: one T-junction run by one whip spider\nOne trial is: one row\nIndependent unit column: animal_id"}]},{"description":"The data description states every animal was run six times on the same evening, so the 36 rows are six repeated (consecutive) runs from each of only six individuals.","observed_value":"6 animals x 6 consecutive runs = 36 rows","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":5,"end_line":6,"quoted_text":"Every animal was run six times on the same evening, so the file holds\nthirty-six runs in total: six consecutive runs for each of the six individuals."}]},{"description":"The workflow computes the p-value with a single exact binomial test whose trial count is the total number of rows (36), a procedure that assumes each row is an independent Bernoulli observation.","observed_value":"binomtest(n_right=25, n_trials=36, 0.5) -> p = 0.0288","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":31,"end_line":35,"quoted_text":"    n_trials = len(trials)\n    n_right = sum(1 for trial in trials if trial[\"arm_chosen\"] == RIGHT_ARM)\n    pooled_share = n_right / n_trials\n    mean_latency = sum(float(trial[\"latency_s\"]) for trial in trials) / n_trials\n    p_value = binomtest(n_right, n_trials, NULL_RATE, alternative=\"two-sided\").pvalue"}]},{"description":"The report explicitly states that every row was treated as one Bernoulli observation in the pooled test.","observed_value":"each of 36 rows treated as an independent observation","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":11,"end_line":13,"quoted_text":"Every trial in the file was treated as one Bernoulli observation. The pooled\ncount of right-arm entries was compared against a chance rate of 0.5 with an\nexact two-sided binomial test (scipy.stats.binomtest)."}]},{"description":"The selected result claims significance at the 0.05 level based on the pooled 36-trial binomial test.","observed_value":"p = 0.0288 on 36 pooled trials","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":32,"end_line":32,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Exact two-sided binomial test on 36 pooled trials with 25 right-arm entries against a chance rate of 0.5 gives p = 0.0288 (pooled share 0.694), a departure from chance at the 0.05 level."}]},{"description":"The report's own per-animal tallies show strong between-animal heterogeneity (shares from 0.333 to 1.000), confirming that repeated rows within an animal are correlated and that pooling inflates the effective sample size. Recomputation at the animal level (t-test on the six shares: t ≈ 1.94, df = 5, p ≈ 0.11; sign test on majority direction: 4 right, 1 left, 1 tie, p = 0.375) does not reach 0.05.","observed_value":"per-animal shares: 1.000, 0.833, 0.833, 0.667, 0.500, 0.333; animal-level p ≈ 0.11 (t-test) / 0.375 (sign test)","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":17,"end_line":24,"quoted_text":"| animal_id | trials | right-arm entries | share |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| AMB-01 | 6 | 6 | 1.000 |\n| AMB-02 | 6 | 5 | 0.833 |\n| AMB-03 | 6 | 5 | 0.833 |\n| AMB-04 | 6 | 4 | 0.667 |\n| AMB-05 | 6 | 3 | 0.500 |\n| AMB-06 | 6 | 2 | 0.333 |"},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":7,"quoted_text":"AMB-01,1,right,38.2\nAMB-01,2,right,41.7\nAMB-01,3,right,35.9\nAMB-01,4,right,44.1\nAMB-01,5,right,39.6\nAMB-01,6,right,42.8"}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The report transparently discloses the repeated-measures structure and per-animal tallies (6 animals, 6 trials each, shares 0.333–1.000), so the pooling is not concealed; however, disclosure does not change the fact that the selected statistical conclusion is computed by a row-independent test on repeated within-animal rows.","observed_value":"report Notes acknowledge 6 animals x 6 trials and per-animal share range","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":36,"end_line":38,"quoted_text":"The 36 trials come from 6 animals, 6 trials each, and the\nper-animal shares span 0.333 to 1.000. Each trial contributes\none observation to the test above."}]},{"description":"The arena was wiped between runs and the arms were swapped halfway through the session, which mitigates scent-trail and side-of-room confounds across runs; but these procedural controls do not make repeated choices by the same individual statistically independent (individual side preference remains, as the per-animal shares show).","observed_value":"arena wiped between runs; arms swapped mid-session","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":7,"end_line":8,"quoted_text":"The arena was wiped down between runs and the two arms were swapped in position\nhalfway through the session."}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I tried to defeat the finding in three ways. (1) Could the rows be independent despite the shared animal_id? No: the data description names animal_id as the independent-unit column, and the per-animal shares (1.000 down to 0.333) show clear between-animal heterogeneity, i.e., positive within-animal correlation, so the 36 rows are not exchangeable independent Bernoulli draws. (2) Could the conclusion survive an animal-level reanalysis, making the pooling harmless in effect? No: a one-sample t-test on the six per-animal right-arm shares against 0.5 gives mean 0.694, sd ≈ 0.245, t ≈ 1.94 with df = 5, p ≈ 0.11; a sign test on majority direction (4 animals majority-right, 1 majority-left, 1 tied) gives p = 0.375. Both exceed 0.05, so the significance claim depends on the pooled row-level test. (3) Could the report be read as claiming only a descriptive pooled share rather than an inferential conclusion? No: the [selected-result] line explicitly reports p = 0.0288 from the 36-trial binomial test and asserts 'a departure from chance at the 0.05 level.' I also verified the arithmetic from data/input.csv: 25 right-arm entries of 36 rows, matching the report, so the issue is in the inferential unit, not a computation error.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]}